**parseCount(text)**

Write `parseCount(text)`: return the whole number written in `text`, or `null` if it isn't one. `"007"` is 7, `"-4"` is -4; `""`, `"   "`, `"3.5"` and `"12abc"` are all `null`.

```js
function parseCount(text) {
  return parseInt(text, 10);
}
```

back:
// Why: `parseInt("12abc")` stops at the first bad character and returns 12;
// `Number("12abc")` is NaN. But `Number("")` and `Number("   ")` are 0, so empty input
// needs its own check.
// Docs: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/isInteger
function parseCount(text) {
  if (text.trim() === "") return null;
  const n = Number(text);
  return Number.isInteger(n) ? n : null;
}

